scp:relatedConcept a owl:ObjectProperty .

scp:resourceLabel a owl:AnnotationProperty .

scp:resourceLink a owl:ObjectProperty .

scp:sec_abstract a scp:ReportSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Abstract"@en ;
    scp:extendedTreatment "This report presents the Semantic Control Plane as a layered framework for enterprise knowledge representation, worked through an illustrative retail supply-chain case. It surveys the field's seminal foundations, the complete W3C standards stack, the layered ontology architecture, and the contemporary knowledge-graph and GraphRAG synthesis. The contribution is an integrated, citation-grounded account in which every concept is illustrated with a concrete example and situated within the formal description-logic decomposition of a knowledge base."@en ;
    scp:sectionOrder "0" .

scp:sec_conclusion a scp:ReportSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "8. Conclusion"@en ;
    scp:extendedTreatment "A faithful semantic representation of an enterprise domain engages the full W3C stack, the full layered ontology architecture, and the seminal literature that defines the field. The retail worked example demonstrates that the framework is coherent and constructible end-to-end, with every concept illustrated by real catalogue data and traceable to a source. The framework does not make adoption cheap or automatic; it makes the representational choices explicit enough that an organization can judge their value for itself."@en ;
    scp:sectionOrder "8" .

scp:sec_discussion a scp:ReportSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "7. Discussion"@en ;
    scp:extendedTreatment "Working the framework through the retail case surfaces a consistent lesson: the value of each layer is realized only in combination with those beneath it, and the retail-specific pressures — multilingual catalogues across many countries, category-dependent policy, the decoupling of supply from store-level demand — are each best represented at a specific layer. The framework's practical payoff is the conversion of an N-squared integration problem into a linear one through foundational alignment, and the conversion of an ungrounded language model into a governable one through graph grounding."@en ;
    scp:sectionOrder "7" .

scp:sec_intro a scp:ReportSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "1. Introduction"@en ;
    scp:extendedTreatment "Multi-domain enterprises accumulate decoupled data faster than they reconcile its meaning, producing semantic drift: the fragmentation of cross-functional meaning across isolated silos (Shadbolt et al., 2006). A large multinational apparel retailer — hundreds of stores across many countries, an e-commerce catalogue of hundreds of thousands of SKUs, and a supplier network — exemplifies the problem, because the same product, store, supplier, and category must mean the same thing across merchandising, logistics, finance, and the storefront. The Semantic Control Plane addresses this by making meaning explicit and shared through a cumulative stack of representational layers. This report develops that framework, grounding each layer in the seminal literature and illustrating each concept with illustrative catalogue data."@en ;
    scp:sectionOrder "1" .

scp:sec_related a scp:ReportSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "2. Background and related work"@en ;
    scp:extendedTreatment "The field has two foundational texts: the vision of a machine-meaningful web (Berners-Lee, Hendler & Lassila, 2001) and the canonical definition of an ontology as an explicit specification of a conceptualization (Gruber, 1993). Uschold and Gruninger (1996) and Guarino (1998) developed the methodology and the layered theory of ontology types. The contemporary synthesis is surveyed definitively by Hogan et al. (2021) and grounded formally by the description-logic treatment of Baader et al. (2017). The current frontier joins knowledge graphs to language models through GraphRAG (Edge et al., 2024; Pan et al., 2024)."@en ;
    scp:sectionOrder "2" .

